==Abstract==

This ELIP defines a browser interface for discovering Liquid wallet providers and invoking the methods and receiving
the events defined by the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile.

Wallets announce providers through a race-free pair of <code>window</code> events. Applications select an announced
provider and interact with it through a minimal JavaScript interface consisting of <code>request</code> and
<code>on</code>. The <code>request</code> method accepts a method name and parameters, then resolves directly to the
method result or rejects with a structured provider error. The <code>on</code> method registers an event listener and
returns an idempotent unsubscribe function.

This ELIP also defines an origin-bound connection model. A connection identifies one Liquid or Elements chain, one
connected account, and the RPC methods and events authorized for the calling origin. Provider discovery exposes no
account state. Applications must obtain user authorization before invoking account-scoped Liquid RPC methods.

The design adapts the transport-agnostic provider model of
[https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-1193 EIP-1193], the multi-provider discovery model of
[https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-6963 EIP-6963], and the privacy-gated connection and capability-negotiation
patterns of [https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-2255 EIP-2255],
[https://cips.cardano.org/cip/CIP-0030 CIP-30], and
[https://github.com/wallet-standard/wallet-standard Wallet Standard]. It does not define a singleton
<code>window.liquid</code> provider and does not claim conformance to any of those standards.

