==Motivation==

The Liquid Wallet RPC Profile defines transport-neutral wallet methods, results, and events. A browser application
additionally needs interoperable answers to the following questions:

* How does an application discover installed Liquid wallets without knowing wallet-specific global names?
* How can multiple installed wallets coexist without racing to overwrite a singleton property on <code>window</code>?
* What JavaScript interface carries RPC calls and events?
* How is private account state withheld until the user authorizes the calling origin?
* How does an application restore, inspect, update, or revoke its connection?
* How are supported capabilities distinguished from capabilities granted to one origin?
* How are provider errors represented independently of an internal browser-extension transport?

Historically, injected wallet interfaces have used singleton globals. A singleton cannot represent multiple installed
wallets without an arbitrary first-loaded or last-loaded winner. Extension initialization order is not user intent,
and a shared mutable property encourages overwrite races. This ELIP therefore makes event-based provider discovery
the only standardized discovery mechanism.

The page-facing interface is deliberately smaller than raw JSON-RPC 2.0. Applications call
<code>request({ method, params })</code> and use the returned Promise as the handle for that invocation. The provider,
acting as the JSON-RPC client when JSON-RPC is used internally, creates correlation identifiers and maps responses back
to Promises. Browser applications do not need to allocate identifiers or process protocol response envelopes.

==Terminology==

{| class="wikitable"
! Term !! Definition
|-
| Application / dapp || A web application that discovers and calls a Liquid wallet provider.
|-
| Wallet || Software that manages Liquid accounts, applies wallet policy, requests user approval, and services provider requests.
|-
| Provider || The JavaScript object exposed by a wallet to an application through the discovery protocol defined by this ELIP.
|-
| Provider instance || One provider object announced within one top-level browsing context.
|-
| Calling origin || The origin of the top-level browsing context in which the provider request is made. The wallet derives this value; application input cannot set it.
|-
| Connection || The wallet-maintained authorization binding one calling origin to one Liquid account, one chain, and a set of granted methods and events.
|-
| Supported capability || A method or event implemented by the wallet.
|-
| Granted permission || A supported method or event that the wallet has authorized the calling origin to use.
|-
| Liquid RPC method || A method defined by the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile.
|-
| Liquid RPC event || An event defined by the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile.
|}

==Specification==

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", and "MAY" in this
document are to be interpreted as described in [https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2119 RFC 2119].

===Scope and relationship to the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile===

This ELIP specifies a browser binding. It does not redefine the parameters, results, approval requirements, or event
payloads of the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile.

A conforming provider MUST expose every Liquid RPC method it supports as a direct <code>request</code> method name.
The provider MUST NOT require a chain-routing or session envelope around a Liquid RPC method. For example, an
application requests a balance as follows:

<pre>
const balance = await provider.request({
  method: "getBalance",
  params: {}
});
</pre>

The Promise MUST resolve to the <code>getBalance</code> result defined by the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile, not to a
JSON-RPC response object or another routing envelope.

Unless the Liquid RPC method explicitly contains an <code>account</code> parameter, the provider MUST apply the method
to the account in the calling origin's current connection. If a method contains an <code>account</code> parameter, that
account MUST equal the account in the current connection or the provider MUST reject the request with error
<code>4100</code>.

===Supported browsing contexts===

Provider discovery MUST be exposed only in a
[https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-secure-contexts/ secure context] whose <code>window</code> is the top-level browsing
context. Loopback origins considered secure by the browser remain eligible.

Wallets MUST NOT expose the discovery protocol directly in a cross-origin child frame. This version of the specification
does not define delegated or embedded-frame access. A future ELIP may define an explicit delegation mechanism.

Documents with opaque origins MUST NOT receive a provider announcement.

===Provider discovery===

Provider discovery uses two DOM event types:

{| class="wikitable"
! Event !! Sender !! Purpose
|-
| <code>liquid:requestProvider</code> || Application || Requests all available Liquid wallets to announce their provider.
|-
| <code>liquid:announceProvider</code> || Wallet || Announces one provider and its display metadata.
|}

This ELIP does not define <code>window.liquid</code>, <code>navigator.liquid</code>, a shared provider array, or any other
singleton provider property. Applications conforming to this ELIP MUST NOT depend on such a property for discovery.
A wallet MAY retain a pre-existing global interface for backwards compatibility, but that interface is outside this
ELIP, MUST NOT be required to use the provider defined here, and MUST NOT suppress or alter this discovery protocol.

====Provider information====

Each announcement MUST contain immutable provider information with the following shape:

<pre>
interface LiquidProviderInfo {
  readonly uuid: string;
  readonly name: string;
  readonly icon: string;
  readonly rdns: string;
}
</pre>

The fields have the following meanings:

{| class="wikitable"
! Field !! Requirements
|-
| <code>uuid</code> || A UUID version 4 value that uniquely identifies this provider instance for the lifetime of the page. It is not a stable wallet identity.
|-
| <code>name</code> || A human-readable wallet name suitable for display in a wallet-selection interface.
|-
| <code>icon</code> || A data URI containing a square SVG, WebP, PNG, or GIF image. Applications MUST render an SVG icon through an <code>img</code> element and MUST NOT insert its markup into the document.
|-
| <code>rdns</code> || A reverse-DNS identifier based on a domain controlled by the wallet publisher, such as <code>io.example.wallet</code>. This value is self-asserted and is not cryptographic proof of wallet identity.
|}

Unknown additional fields in <code>LiquidProviderInfo</code> MUST be ignored by applications.

====Provider detail====

An announcement carries the provider information and provider object together:

<pre>
interface LiquidProviderDetail {
  readonly info: LiquidProviderInfo;
  readonly provider: LiquidProvider;
}
</pre>

The wallet MUST freeze the <code>info</code> and <code>detail</code> objects with <code>Object.freeze</code> before
announcing them. The provider object itself MUST contain no secrets and MUST be treated by the wallet as controlled by
an adversarial page.

====Announcement protocol====

The announcement event is a <code>CustomEvent</code> whose type is <code>liquid:announceProvider</code> and whose
<code>detail</code> is a <code>LiquidProviderDetail</code>:

<pre>
interface LiquidAnnounceProviderEvent extends CustomEvent {
  readonly type: "liquid:announceProvider";
  readonly detail: LiquidProviderDetail;
}
</pre>

The request event is an <code>Event</code> whose type is <code>liquid:requestProvider</code>:

<pre>
interface LiquidRequestProviderEvent extends Event {
  readonly type: "liquid:requestProvider";
}
</pre>

When a provider becomes ready, the wallet MUST:

# create one immutable <code>LiquidProviderDetail</code> for the provider instance;
# add a listener for <code>liquid:requestProvider</code> that re-announces that same detail; and
# dispatch <code>liquid:announceProvider</code> once immediately.

An application MUST add its <code>liquid:announceProvider</code> listener before dispatching
<code>liquid:requestProvider</code>. It SHOULD retain the listener for the lifetime of the page so that wallets that
become available later can be discovered.

An application MUST de-duplicate repeated announcements by <code>uuid</code>. A repeated announcement from a conforming
wallet with the same <code>uuid</code> MUST refer to the same provider object for the lifetime of the page. An application
MUST retain the first valid announcement for a <code>uuid</code> and ignore a later announcement with that
<code>uuid</code> if it contains a different provider object or provider information.

The discovery protocol does not select a default wallet. The application or user selects one of the announced provider
instances and retains that <code>provider</code> object for subsequent calls.

====Discovery example====

The following non-normative example discovers providers without an external library:

<pre>
const providers = new Map();

window.addEventListener("liquid:announceProvider", (event) => {
  const detail = event.detail;
  if (!providers.has(detail.info.uuid)) {
    providers.set(detail.info.uuid, detail);
  }
});

window.dispatchEvent(new Event("liquid:requestProvider"));
</pre>

===Provider interface===

A conforming provider MUST implement the following JavaScript interface:

<pre>
interface LiquidProviderRequestArguments {
  readonly method: string;
  readonly params?: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;
}

interface LiquidProviderEventArguments {
  readonly event: string;
  readonly listener: (payload: unknown) => void;
}

interface LiquidProvider {
  request(args: LiquidProviderRequestArguments): Promise<unknown>;
  on(args: LiquidProviderEventArguments): () => void;
}
</pre>

====Request semantics====

<code>request</code> MUST return a Promise for every invocation. If <code>args</code> is not an object,
<code>args.method</code> is not a non-empty string, or <code>params</code> is present but is not a non-null, non-array
object, the returned Promise MUST reject with <code>-32600</code>. Parameters MUST contain only JSON-serializable values.

For each valid invocation, <code>request</code> MUST return a Promise that:

* resolves exactly once to the result defined by the requested method; or
* rejects exactly once with a <code>LiquidProviderError</code>.

The Promise MUST NOT resolve to a JSON-RPC success object, a JSON-RPC failure object, or an object whose sole purpose is
to repeat the requested method, account, or chain.

<code>LiquidProviderRequestArguments</code> is not a
[https://www.jsonrpc.org/specification JSON-RPC 2.0] Request Object. When an implementation uses JSON-RPC internally,
the provider is the JSON-RPC client: it MUST assign a unique <code>id</code>, set
<code>jsonrpc</code> to <code>"2.0"</code>, correlate the response, then resolve or reject the public Promise. The
application MUST NOT supply the transport correlation identifier.

If an application nevertheless includes an <code>id</code> field in <code>args</code>, the provider MUST ignore it for
correlation and MUST NOT forward it as the underlying JSON-RPC identifier.

For example, the provider may map:

<pre>
provider.request({ method: "getBalance", params: {} })
</pre>

to an internal request such as:

<pre>
{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": "provider-generated-42",
  "method": "getBalance",
  "params": {}
}
</pre>

Every call to <code>request</code> expects a result or error. This interface does not expose JSON-RPC notification or
batch-request semantics. Wallet-to-application notifications are delivered through <code>on</code>.

An implementation MAY service independent requests concurrently. It MUST preserve the association between each Promise
and its invocation regardless of response order.

====Event subscription semantics====

<code>on</code> registers one listener for one supported event and MUST return an unsubscribe function for that exact
subscription.

The unsubscribe function MUST be idempotent. After it is first called, the provider MUST NOT invoke that subscription's
listener for subsequently emitted events. Registering the same listener more than once creates distinct subscriptions;
each returned unsubscribe function removes only its own subscription.

The provider MUST isolate listener failures. An exception thrown by one listener MUST NOT prevent other listeners from
receiving the event and MUST NOT alter wallet state.

The provider MAY allow an application to subscribe to a supported permission-controlled Liquid RPC event before a
connection is granted, but it MUST NOT deliver that event until the current connection grants it. Subscribing to
<code>wallet_connectionChanged</code> never requires an event permission.

Malformed subscription arguments MUST cause <code>on</code> to throw a <code>TypeError</code> synchronously. An unsupported
event MUST cause <code>on</code> to throw a <code>LiquidProviderError</code> with code <code>4200</code> synchronously. The
returned unsubscribe function itself MUST NOT throw solely because it has already been called.

===Provider errors===

Every rejected provider request MUST reject with an <code>Error</code> implementing:

<pre>
interface LiquidProviderError extends Error {
  readonly code: number;
  readonly data?: unknown;
}
</pre>

<code>message</code> MUST be a concise human-readable description. <code>code</code> MUST be an integer.
<code>data</code>, when present, MUST be JSON-serializable and MUST NOT contain secrets or wallet-internal diagnostic
information unsafe to disclose to the calling origin.

Providers MUST use the following codes where applicable:

{| class="wikitable"
! Code !! Name !! Meaning
|-
| <code>4001</code> || User Rejected Request || The user rejected the request or approval.
|-
| <code>4100</code> || Unauthorized || The origin, account, method, event, or requested disclosure is not authorized.
|-
| <code>4200</code> || Unsupported Capability || The method or event is defined by a recognized profile but is not supported by this provider.
|-
| <code>4900</code> || Provider Disconnected || The provider cannot communicate with the wallet backend and cannot service requests.
|-
| <code>4901</code> || Chain Unavailable || The provider is operational but cannot service requests for the requested or connected chain.
|-
| <code>-32600</code> || Invalid Request || The request arguments do not form a valid provider request.
|-
| <code>-32601</code> || Method Not Found || The method name is not defined by this ELIP, the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile, or another profile advertised by the provider.
|-
| <code>-32602</code> || Invalid Parameters || The supplied parameters are invalid for the requested method.
|-
| <code>-32603</code> || Internal Error || The provider or wallet encountered an internal error that cannot be represented more specifically.
|}

If a Liquid RPC method specification mandates a more specific error code, the provider MUST preserve that code unless
doing so would disclose information that the method or wallet policy requires it to withhold.

===Capabilities===

The <code>wallet_getCapabilities</code> method reports the provider interface version and supported public surface. It
MUST be callable without an active connection and MUST NOT prompt the user.

Request:

<pre>
{
  "method": "wallet_getCapabilities",
  "params": {}
}
</pre>

Result:

<pre>
interface LiquidProviderCapabilities {
  readonly browserProviderVersion: string;
  readonly methods: readonly string[];
  readonly events: readonly string[];
}
</pre>

<code>browserProviderVersion</code> MUST be a semantic version of the interface defined by this ELIP. Implementations
of this version MUST return <code>"1.0.0"</code>. <code>methods</code> MUST contain every Liquid RPC and
<code>wallet_*</code> method that the provider supports. <code>events</code> MUST contain every event the provider can
emit, including <code>wallet_connectionChanged</code>.

A provider conforming to this version MUST support and advertise <code>wallet_getCapabilities</code>,
<code>wallet_connect</code>, <code>wallet_getConnection</code>, and <code>wallet_disconnect</code>, and MUST support and
advertise <code>wallet_connectionChanged</code>. These browser lifecycle capabilities are available independently of
the Liquid RPC method and event permissions granted to an origin.

The <code>methods</code> and <code>events</code> arrays MUST NOT contain duplicate values. Their order has no semantic
meaning.

Capabilities describe what the provider can implement, not what the calling origin is permitted to use. The result
MUST NOT contain account identifiers, chain selection, wallet lock state, balances, or other user-specific state.

Applications SHOULD feature-detect methods and events from this result rather than infer support from
<code>name</code> or <code>rdns</code>.

===Connection model===

The wallet MUST maintain at most one active connection for each pair of calling origin and provider. A connection binds
the origin to exactly one Liquid account and one Liquid or Elements chain.

The wallet MUST derive and authenticate the calling origin outside page-controlled request parameters. A provider
request MUST NOT accept an <code>origin</code> parameter that overrides the authenticated origin.

The wallet MAY persist connections across navigation, page reload, browser restart, or wallet restart. Persisted
connections MUST remain bound to the same origin and MUST be returned by <code>wallet_getConnection</code> only to that
origin.

The wallet's general account or chain selection UI MUST NOT silently change an existing dapp connection. A connection
changes only when:

* the application requests an updated connection and the user approves it;
* the user explicitly changes the connection for that origin in wallet-controlled UI;
* the connected account or chain becomes unavailable; or
* the connection is revoked or expires.

The connection has the following shape:

<pre>
interface LiquidConnectionPermissions {
  readonly methods: readonly string[];
  readonly events: readonly string[];
}

interface LiquidConnection {
  readonly accountIdentifier: string;
  readonly chainId: string;
  readonly policyAssetId: string;
  readonly permissions: LiquidConnectionPermissions;
}
</pre>

<code>accountIdentifier</code>, <code>chainId</code>, and <code>policyAssetId</code> MUST use the identifier forms
defined by ELIP-0144 and the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile. The chain component of all three values MUST agree.

<code>permissions.methods</code> and <code>permissions.events</code> MUST contain the exact current grant, not merely
the application's original request. Neither array may contain duplicate values, and array order has no semantic
meaning. The permissions MUST NOT contain browser lifecycle methods or <code>wallet_connectionChanged</code>, because
those capabilities do not require a connection grant.

Granting a method authorizes the application to request it. A grant does not require the wallet to execute the method
without further user interaction. The wallet MUST still apply every per-call approval, transaction review, descriptor
disclosure, identity, signing, or wallet-policy requirement imposed by the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile.

===<code>wallet_connect</code>===

<code>wallet_connect</code> requests or updates the calling origin's connection.

Parameters:

<pre>
interface LiquidConnectParams {
  readonly chains?: readonly string[];
  readonly methods: readonly string[];
  readonly events?: readonly string[];
}
</pre>

{| class="wikitable"
! Field !! Required !! Meaning
|-
| <code>chains</code> || No || Acceptable ELIP-0144 chain identifiers. If omitted, the application accepts any Liquid or Elements chain supported by the wallet.
|-
| <code>methods</code> || Yes || Liquid RPC methods for which the application requests permission. The array MUST contain at least one method and MUST NOT contain browser lifecycle methods.
|-
| <code>events</code> || No || Permission-controlled Liquid RPC events the application wants to receive. <code>wallet_connectionChanged</code> MUST NOT be requested because it is always available.
|}

The wallet MUST reject malformed or duplicate entries with <code>-32602</code>. If <code>chains</code> is present, it
MUST contain at least one syntactically valid chain identifier. Unknown method or event names MAY be ignored when at
least one requested method is recognized, but they MUST NOT appear in the grant.

The wallet and user select one account and one acceptable chain. The wallet MAY grant a subset of the requested methods
and events. It MUST NOT grant a method or event that the application did not request during that invocation, except a
permission already present in the origin's existing connection.

A successful call MUST grant at least one method requested by the application. Declining every requested method MUST
reject the request with <code>4001</code> and MUST NOT create an empty connection.

On success, the method returns the complete current <code>LiquidConnection</code>.

If the existing connection already uses an acceptable chain and its grant is equal to or wider than the request, the
wallet SHOULD return it without prompting again. Creating a connection, adding permissions, or changing the connected
account or chain MUST require affirmative user approval. Rejecting a connection update MUST leave the previous
connection unchanged unless the user separately revokes it through an explicit wallet-controlled action.

If none of the requested chains is supported or available, the provider MUST reject with <code>4901</code>. If the user
rejects the connection, the provider MUST reject with <code>4001</code>. If no requested method is supported, the
provider MUST reject with <code>4200</code>.

An application MUST inspect the returned grant and MUST NOT assume that every requested method or event was granted.

Example:

<pre>
const connection = await provider.request({
  method: "wallet_connect",
  params: {
    chains: ["bip122:1466275836220db2944ca059a3a10ef6"],
    methods: ["getBalance", "sendTransfer"],
    events: ["bip122_walletDescriptorChanged"]
  }
});
</pre>

===<code>wallet_getConnection</code>===

<code>wallet_getConnection</code> returns the calling origin's active connection without prompting the user.

Request:

<pre>
{
  "method": "wallet_getConnection",
  "params": {}
}
</pre>

The result MUST be the complete current <code>LiquidConnection</code>, or <code>null</code> if no active connection
exists. A wallet MUST NOT return <code>null</code> solely because it is locked. It MAY return non-secret connection
metadata while locked if wallet policy permits it; otherwise it MUST reject with <code>4100</code>. It MUST NOT expose a
connection belonging to another origin.

Applications SHOULD subscribe to <code>wallet_connectionChanged</code> before calling
<code>wallet_getConnection</code>, so a connection change between initialization steps is not missed.

===<code>wallet_disconnect</code>===

<code>wallet_disconnect</code> revokes the calling origin's active connection.

Request:

<pre>
{
  "method": "wallet_disconnect",
  "params": {}
}
</pre>

The method MUST be idempotent and MUST resolve to <code>null</code>. If a connection existed, the wallet MUST revoke its
method and event grants before resolving and MUST emit <code>wallet_connectionChanged</code> with <code>null</code>.

===Authorization of Liquid RPC methods===

Before servicing a Liquid RPC method, the provider MUST verify that:

# the calling origin has an active connection;
# the requested method appears in <code>connection.permissions.methods</code>;
# the method applies to <code>connection.chainId</code>;
# any explicit <code>account</code> parameter equals <code>connection.accountIdentifier</code>; and
# all method-specific validation and wallet policy requirements pass.

Failure of the first four authorization checks MUST reject with <code>4100</code> except that an unavailable connected
chain MAY reject with <code>4901</code>. Parameter syntax failures MUST reject with <code>-32602</code>.

===Events===

====<code>wallet_connectionChanged</code>====

<code>wallet_connectionChanged</code> notifies an application that its origin-bound connection changed. Its payload is
the complete new <code>LiquidConnection</code>, or <code>null</code> if the connection no longer exists.

The wallet MUST emit this event after:

* a successful connection request creates a connection or changes the account, chain, or grant;
* the user changes this origin's connection in wallet-controlled UI;
* the connection is revoked or expires; or
* the connected account or chain becomes unavailable and the connection is removed.

The wallet MUST NOT emit this event merely because the wallet's general UI changes its currently displayed account or
chain while the origin-bound connection remains unchanged.

This event reports authorization state. It MUST NOT use <code>null</code> merely because the provider temporarily cannot
communicate with its backend, the wallet is locked, or an individual request fails. Provider transport failures are
reported with error <code>4900</code>.

====Liquid RPC events====

The provider MUST deliver a Liquid RPC event only if:

* the event is supported;
* the calling origin has an active connection;
* the event appears in <code>connection.permissions.events</code>; and
* the event data belongs to the connected account and chain.

The event name and payload MUST match the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile. The provider MUST NOT add a JSON-RPC notification
envelope, provider identifier, account-routing envelope, or chain-routing envelope around the event payload.

===Versioning and extensibility===

The provider interface version follows semantic versioning:

* a major version changes incompatible provider, discovery, connection, or error semantics;
* a minor version adds backward-compatible browser methods, events, or fields; and
* a patch version clarifies behavior without changing the public interface.

Applications MUST ignore unknown fields in result and event objects unless the method defining the object states
otherwise. Providers MUST reject unknown methods with <code>-32601</code>. New optional Liquid RPC methods and events
are feature-detected through <code>wallet_getCapabilities</code>.

The <code>wallet_*</code> method and event namespace is reserved for standardized provider lifecycle and control
capabilities. An implementation MUST NOT invent wallet-specific methods in this namespace. A future ELIP MAY add or
adopt additional standardized capabilities in it.

==Rationale==

===EIP-1193 provider model===

[https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-1193 EIP-1193] establishes a small, transport- and protocol-agnostic provider whose
<code>request</code> method resolves directly to an RPC method result and rejects with a structured provider error. This
ELIP adopts that request and error model because it keeps JSON-RPC transport details out of application code and allows
the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile to evolve by adding method names.

This ELIP does not claim EIP-1193 conformance. It defines Liquid methods, connection semantics, and events, and it uses a
different subscription-removal interface.

===Unsubscribe function instead of <code>removeListener</code>===

EIP-1193 inherits the Node.js <code>EventEmitter</code> convention of <code>on</code> plus
<code>removeListener</code>. That convention requires applications to retain the exact listener function reference.
Anonymous listeners and wrapped listeners are therefore easy to register but difficult to remove correctly.

Returning an unsubscribe function binds cleanup to one precise subscription, handles duplicate listener registrations
without ambiguity, and composes with component lifecycle APIs. Idempotency also makes repeated cleanup safe. This ELIP
therefore defines <code>on({ event, listener }) =&gt; unsubscribe</code> and does not define
<code>removeListener</code>. This cleanup shape is also used by
[https://github.com/wallet-standard/wallet-standard Wallet Standard].

===EIP-6963 discovery model===

[https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-6963 EIP-6963] solves load-order and namespace collisions among injected providers
by using paired request and announcement events. This ELIP adopts that event choreography and its
<code>uuid</code>/<code>name</code>/<code>icon</code>/<code>rdns</code> provider metadata, while using Liquid-specific
event names and a Liquid provider type.

EIP-6963 is a discovery layer rather than a replacement for the provider interface. Similarly, discovery under this
ELIP yields a <code>LiquidProvider</code>; applications then retain that provider and call its methods.

===No singleton global===

A property such as <code>window.liquid</code> can refer to only one provider. If multiple extensions assign it, browser
load order determines the winner unless wallets overwrite one another. Neither outcome expresses user choice. Defining
both a singleton and event discovery would also create two permanent integration paths.

Because this interface begins without a deployed legacy global, this ELIP standardizes discovery only and leaves wallet
selection to the application and user.

===CIP-30 connection model===

[https://cips.cardano.org/cip/CIP-0030 CIP-30] separates public wallet metadata from an authorized account API obtained
after <code>enable()</code>. It also distinguishes extensions supported by a wallet from extensions enabled for a dapp,
and distinguishes connection consent from per-call signing approval.

This ELIP adopts those separations while retaining one stable, extensible provider object. Provider discovery and
<code>wallet_getCapabilities</code> reveal no account state. <code>wallet_connect</code> establishes the authorized
account context. Sensitive Liquid RPC methods continue to apply their own per-call approval rules.

===Method-scoped permissions and EIP-2255===

[https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-2255 EIP-2255] models wallet permissions as origin-scoped capabilities and keeps a
permission grant distinct from a later request that exercises it. This ELIP adopts those properties, but does not adopt
EIP-2255's Ethereum-specific <code>wallet_requestPermissions</code> request or caveat representation.

Liquid browser applications need an account, chain, method grant, and event grant as one coherent authorization result.
<code>wallet_connect</code> therefore returns that complete connection directly. The representation is intentionally
specific to the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile rather than a generic capability language.

===CAIP-25 sessions and CAIP-27 invocation===

[https://standards.chainagnostic.org/CAIPs/caip-25 CAIP-25] defines chain-agnostic session authorization using scopes
that can cover multiple networks, accounts, methods, and notifications.
[https://standards.chainagnostic.org/CAIPs/caip-27 CAIP-27] defines <code>wallet_invokeMethod</code>, a routing method
whose parameters contain a target chain, a nested RPC method and parameters, and a session identifier when required.
Those abstractions are useful when one agent brokers multiple protocol namespaces, chains, or concurrent sessions.
CAIP-27 standardizes that routing envelope; it does not by itself define the nested Liquid method dialect, browser
provider object, discovery mechanism, or subscription interface.

This ELIP has a narrower browser contract: a provider connection already selects one Liquid account and chain, and the
Liquid Wallet RPC Profile supplies an unambiguous method dialect. Making CAIP-27 the only invocation path would repeat
the connected chain on every request, add nested result and error envelopes, and make ordinary Liquid calls depend on a
broader session stack. Direct methods are therefore the mandatory public path.

This choice does not make CAIP compatibility impossible. A future ELIP may define an optional CAIP-25/27 adapter or
additional provider capabilities for multi-chain or multi-session use. Such an adapter should be advertised through
capability discovery and should not change the direct invocation semantics defined here.

===Direct Liquid RPC invocation===

The Liquid Wallet RPC Profile already assigns unique method names and specifies account and chain behavior through the
transport session. Requiring every browser call to be nested in an additional routing method would add redundant syntax
for the common case of one account and chain per origin. This ELIP therefore invokes Liquid RPC methods directly and
stores the account and chain in the provider connection.

===One account and chain per connection===

A singular connection makes the implicit account context in the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile deterministic. It also
prevents a wallet-wide account or network selection from becoming shared mutable state among unrelated applications.
Future standards can add explicit multi-account request forms without changing the meaning of this version's implicit
account context.

===Full connection snapshots===

Separate account, chain, and permission events can be observed in different orders and briefly create an impossible
combination of state. <code>wallet_connectionChanged</code> therefore carries one complete snapshot. Applications can
replace their previous connection state atomically.

===Provider-owned correlation identifiers===

JSON-RPC 2.0 requires the client that originates a Request Object to establish its identifier. In this interface, the
provider originates the underlying Request Object and handles its Response Object. The application is a consumer of the
provider's Promise-based client API. Provider ownership prevents identifier collisions among unrelated page libraries
and preserves the provider's freedom to change its internal transport.

===Permissions are not automatic approval===

A method grant means that an origin may ask the wallet to perform that method. It does not mean that a wallet must sign,
disclose descriptors, derive identity material, or transfer funds without further review. This distinction permits a
single understandable connection prompt while retaining the method-specific protections required for Liquid's
confidential and signing operations.

==Security Considerations==

===Adversarial page environment===

The provider object is exposed to application scripts and MUST be treated as adversarial input by the wallet. The
provider MUST contain no private keys, private blinding keys, authentication tokens, view material, or other secrets.
Wallet logic and key material MUST remain isolated from the page. The wallet MUST validate every request again at its
trusted boundary and MUST NOT rely on the page-visible provider object for authorization.

===Origin authentication===

The calling origin is a security principal. A page can forge fields in its own JavaScript messages, so the wallet MUST
derive the origin from an authenticated browser or extension boundary. The origin comparison MUST include scheme, host,
and port. Connections and event delivery MUST be isolated between origins.

For framed or delegated access, comparing only the top-level URL, accepting a caller-provided origin, or trusting an
unauthenticated <code>postMessage</code> field is insufficient. This version avoids that ambiguity by limiting provider
discovery to secure top-level contexts.

===Discovery spoofing and wallet metadata===

Any page script can dispatch a DOM event and can imitate provider metadata. <code>uuid</code>, <code>name</code>,
<code>icon</code>, and <code>rdns</code> are discovery and display data, not cryptographic identity. Applications MUST
NOT treat them as proof that a provider is genuine. Wallet-controlled approval UI MUST display the authenticated calling
origin and MUST remain authoritative for security-sensitive decisions.

Applications MUST render provider icons as images. They MUST NOT execute or insert SVG markup from an announcement.

===Privacy and fingerprinting===

Discovery reveals that a wallet provider is installed. <code>wallet_getCapabilities</code> may reveal additional
implementation characteristics. Wallets SHOULD keep pre-connection metadata stable and minimal. Discovery and capability
results MUST NOT reveal whether an account exists, which account or chain is selected, wallet balances, wallet lock
state, device model, or persisted connection state for another origin.

Account identifiers, descriptors, balances, UTXOs, identity material, and transaction data MUST remain protected by the
connection and method-specific policies.

===Request completion and timeouts===

For a request that may sign, broadcast, disclose sensitive data, or otherwise change state, the provider MUST NOT reject
with a timeout or cancellation result while allowing the wallet to complete the operation later without an authoritative
result path to the application. Before reporting such a request as canceled, the trusted wallet backend MUST guarantee
that the operation can no longer complete. Otherwise the provider MUST continue waiting or reject only with an error that
accurately communicates the operation's uncertain state.

===Event isolation and resource limits===

Wallets SHOULD limit subscriptions and request rates per origin. One listener's exception MUST NOT affect wallet state
or other listeners. Revoking a connection MUST stop delivery of every permission-controlled event before the revocation
is reported as complete.

==Backwards Compatibility==

This ELIP introduces a new browser provider and discovery interface. It does not alter the Liquid consensus protocol,
existing JSON-RPC transports, or wallet-specific browser APIs.

There is no standardized <code>window.liquid</code> interface to preserve. Wallets with pre-existing global interfaces
may continue to expose them for backwards compatibility, but those interfaces remain outside this ELIP. Applications
conforming to this ELIP discover and use providers only through <code>liquid:requestProvider</code> and
<code>liquid:announceProvider</code>.

==Reference TypeScript Definitions==

The following definitions summarize the browser-specific surface. The Liquid Wallet RPC Profile supplies the strongly
typed request, result, and domain-event mappings composed with these types by an implementation library.

<pre>
type Unsubscribe = () => void;

interface LiquidProviderInfo {
  readonly uuid: string;
  readonly name: string;
  readonly icon: string;
  readonly rdns: string;
}

interface LiquidProviderRequestArguments {
  readonly method: string;
  readonly params?: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;
}

interface LiquidProviderEventArguments<Payload = unknown> {
  readonly event: string;
  readonly listener: (payload: Payload) => void;
}

interface LiquidProviderError extends Error {
  readonly code: number;
  readonly data?: unknown;
}

interface LiquidProvider {
  request(args: LiquidProviderRequestArguments): Promise<unknown>;
  on(args: LiquidProviderEventArguments): Unsubscribe;
}

interface LiquidProviderDetail {
  readonly info: LiquidProviderInfo;
  readonly provider: LiquidProvider;
}

interface LiquidProviderCapabilities {
  readonly browserProviderVersion: "1.0.0";
  readonly methods: readonly string[];
  readonly events: readonly string[];
}

interface LiquidConnectionPermissions {
  readonly methods: readonly string[];
  readonly events: readonly string[];
}

interface LiquidConnection {
  readonly accountIdentifier: string;
  readonly chainId: string;
  readonly policyAssetId: string;
  readonly permissions: LiquidConnectionPermissions;
}

interface LiquidConnectParams {
  readonly chains?: readonly string[];
  readonly methods: readonly string[];
  readonly events?: readonly string[];
}
</pre>

==Test Cases==

A conforming reference test suite SHOULD cover at least the following cases:

# The wallet is ready before the application listener; the request event causes discovery.
# The application listener is ready before the wallet; the initial announcement causes discovery.
# Two wallets announce without overwriting or suppressing one another.
# Re-announcing one provider uses the same <code>uuid</code> and provider object.
# Discovery and provider use do not depend on a <code>window.liquid</code> property, whether or not a legacy property is present.
# Every <code>request</code> invocation returns a Promise, including a malformed invocation that rejects.
# Concurrent requests resolve to their own results even when responses arrive out of order.
# A caller cannot supply or collide with the provider's internal JSON-RPC identifier.
# An unsubscribe function removes exactly one subscription and remains safe when called twice.
# An unsupported event causes <code>on</code> to throw code <code>4200</code> synchronously.
# A listener exception does not prevent another listener from receiving the same event.
# Capabilities include every required lifecycle method and event without duplicate values or account state.
# Account-scoped RPC methods reject with <code>4100</code> before connection.
# A connection result contains one internally consistent account, chain, and policy asset.
# A new connection's partial grant contains only methods and events requested by the application and at least one method.
# Rejecting a connection expansion leaves the previous connection unchanged.
# An explicit account parameter that differs from the connected account rejects with <code>4100</code>.
# Revocation stops permission-controlled event delivery before resolving.
# Connections and events are isolated between two origins.
# Provider discovery is not exposed directly to a cross-origin child frame.
# Wallet UI account changes do not silently mutate an unrelated origin connection.
# <code>wallet_connectionChanged</code> delivers one complete new snapshot or <code>null</code>.

==Reference Implementation==

Initial TypeScript definitions for the Liquid Wallet RPC Profile are available in
[https://github.com/Resolvr-io/apogee/blob/main/src/provider/liquid-rpc.ts Apogee]. Apogee also contains an experimental
browser-provider implementation of this ELIP.
